INT. PIANO RESTORATION WORKSHOP - DAY

A high-ceilinged loft with north light. Disemboweled pianos stand open on every side, their strings like harps, their hammers laid out on felt cloths in long neat rows. Sawdust and varnish and old ivory.

NOELLE FAIRCHILD, fifties, the restorer, in a leather apron and reading glasses pushed up into grey curls, bends over a half-dismantled Steinway, a tuning lever in her fist.

DELPHINE ARCHAMBAULT, thirties, her apprentice, stands across the keybed holding a cloth, the curve of her jaw set hard.

LEO FINCH, sixties, the piano's owner, in a rumpled tweed jacket, sits on a bench by the window watching them both, a cane across his knees.

NOELLE
Delphine, the damper felt.

DELPHINE
Which side?

NOELLE
The left. The one that's flat.

Delphine hands her the felt. She does not let go at once. Noelle gives it one sharp tug, and Delphine lets go, and neither looks at the other, and Leo studies the dust in the light.

LEO
You two have been at this since September.

NOELLE
Fourteen weeks.

DELPHINE
Fifteen.

LEO
It was my wife's piano. She played it every morning for forty years.

NOELLE
I know.

LEO
Delphine knows too. She came to the funeral.

DELPHINE
I did.

Noelle sets down the lever. She straightens slowly, one hand on the small of her back, and looks at Delphine for the first time in the scene. Delphine meets her gaze and does not blink.

NOELLE
She wanted the middle C re-voiced.

LEO
Who did?

NOELLE
She did. Your wife.

DELPHINE
She told me. She told me last spring, the day I started.

NOELLE
She told you.

DELPHINE
She didn't want to hurt you.

Leo gets up. The cane rattles on the floor. He crosses to the keyboard and presses middle C. A flat, dull note.

LEO
Hurt me how?

DELPHINE
She said it had been out of tune for years and she played it anyway because you loved it.

Leo presses it again, harder. The note thuds and dies. He keeps his finger on the key. His shoulders bend.

NOELLE
Leo.

LEO
She never said.

NOELLE
She never would.

He lifts his hand. Delphine steps forward as if to touch his arm and stops. Noelle takes up the tuning lever and sets it against the pin, and nods once at Delphine, and Delphine nods back, and together they begin, slowly, to bring the note home.
